---
name: naming
description: "Choose concrete, short names in bp3's existing vocabulary for quests, exps, skills, plans, engines, and CLI verbs."
---
<!-- GENERATED from skills/naming/SKILL.md by: bp3 skills install --harness=muse. Edit the source, then re-run. -->

# Naming

Use when a human, Controller, or Worker in bp3 must name or
rename something: a quest slug, exp, skill, plan (`quest_type`),
engine, CLI verb, file, or state. Invoked by name (`/naming`,
`$naming`). One output: one recommended name, with the vocabulary
it reuses. A shortlist only when two names genuinely tie.

Use `quest` for framing a quest (it delegates naming here),
`using-bp3` for operating the CLI, and general judgment for
ordinary code identifiers with no bp3 meaning.

## The vocabulary (reuse before inventing)

- Planning: Quest, exp, Run. Lifecycle: proposed, active,
  landed, canceled. Never archived, cancelled, aborted, killed.
- Moves: handoff, grade, verify, land, block, resume. Stages: author,
  grade, fix, ready, landed, blocked, canceled.
- Dimensions: `quest_type` (the plan: default is implemented;
  design, mvp, refactor, frontend, backend are proposed), `engine` (who executes: single,
  master-subagents, panes, workflows).
- Artifacts: QUEST.md, fsm.yaml, state.yaml, ledger.jsonl,
  tape.log, notes.md, experiments/, runs/.
- Ceremony lanes: micro, standard, high-blast-radius.

## Procedure

1. Name the kind of thing and where it will be read (CLI, file
   path, quest doc, chat). The same concept may need the kind's
   local spelling: kebab-case for slugs and files, lowercase
   words for verbs and stages.
2. Search the vocabulary above and the repo for the concept's
   existing word. If a word exists, use it, even imperfectly.
3. If no word exists, coin the shortest concrete one: a noun for
   things, a verb for moves. Two syllables beat three; a word a
   newcomer guesses correctly beats a clever one.
4. Check the name against its neighbors: it must not collide
   with, abbreviate, or synonym-shadow an existing term.
5. Say the name, the word it reuses or why none fit, and stop.

## Rules

- One concept, one word, one spelling, everywhere: files, CLI,
  docs, and chat. `canceled` in a status and `cancelled` in a
  stage is a bug, not a dialect.
- Concrete over abstract: prefer what the thing does or is
  (tape, ledger, handoff) over machinery metaphors (flavor,
  harness adapter, pipeline stage) unless the metaphor is
  already the vocabulary.
- No new planning words. Task, Milestone, workflow, and graph
  terms stay out; Quest and exp are the planning vocabulary.
- Renames are explicit edits the human approves, with every
  reference updated in the same change. A rename that leaves a
  synonym behind in docs has not happened.
- Instructions only: this skill decides words, never state.
=== harness link help ===
bp3 harness link - one-time link binding (mvp.1):
bp3 harness link mint --kind node|edge --graph <g> --node <n> [--edge <e>] [--parent-run-id <id>] [--depth <n>] [--desk <d>] [--harness <h>] [--model <m>] [--thinking <t>] [--cycle <n>] [--run-id <id>]
bp3 harness link claim <token> [--session-id <s>]
bp3 harness link lookup --session-id <s> (or --run-id <id>)
bp3 harness link cleanup --graph <g>
Mint writes runs/<run_id>.json (state minted; creator: parent run_id, depth, harness, model, minted_at, token_hash) and links/<token_hash> -> run_id, and returns the one-time token. Depth beyond the machine depth cap (config thresholds.depth_cap, default 3) refuses E_DEPTH. Claim is a compare-and-swap on an O_EXCL claim marker: exactly one winner completes the run config (state claimed; claim: session_id when offered, pid, cwd, claimed_at), writes the by-session index and the desk context; a second claim refuses E_LINK_SPENT naming first_claim (session_id, pid, ts) forever. Session-id discovery: --session-id, else BP3_SESSION_ID, MUSE_SESSION_ID/MUSE_THREAD_ID, PI_SESSION_ID/PI_SESSION_FILE; with none offered the claim still succeeds on pid+cwd. Lookup resolves session-id to run-id, graph, node, edge in one call. Cleanup removes only still-minted run configs of one graph. Run config writes are single-write temp+fsync+rename; a torn run config is never read as valid.
Example: bp3 harness link claim bp3link_7f3a
=== bp3 e help ===
bp3 e - claimed edge door (mvp.2): context is resolved, never carried:
bp3 e                                    print the resolved context, its source rung (session|desk), state, and legal edges
bp3 e <edge> [flags]                     take the claimed run's edge: handoff grade verify land block resume (same flags as bp3 q <quest> u <exp> <edge>)
Resolution: session claim first, desk context second, checked against cwd; no rung refuses E_NO_CONTEXT, an ambiguous desk refuses E_NO_CONTEXT naming the live run_ids. Env BP3_RUN_ID is a hint only: a mismatch warns W_CONTEXT_ENV_MISMATCH and the claim wins; an unclaimed explicit edge call warns W_CONTEXT_UNCLAIMED.
Example: bp3 e handoff --commit abc123 --reason "ready"
=== q help ===
bp3 q - quest/exp state. Doors:
bp3 q c|r|d ...              quest door (create, read, delete)
bp3 q <quest> c|r|u|d ...    exp door for one quest
bp3 q <quest> diagram ...    generated graph diagram from the live rollup (mvp.4)
bp3 q icebox r [term...]     search frozen icebox entries (read-only)
bp3 e [<edge> ...]           claimed edge door: resolved context, no names typed (mvp.2)
bp3 harness desk <verb> ...  desks (machine-local); bp3 worktree is its documented alias
bp3 config [--source]        view the machine-local config store (<git-common-dir>/bp3/)
bp3 q hooks                  harness hook emitter (fail-open)
Run bp3 q <door> -h for that door's verbs, edges, and flags.
